#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681} :activity/id #uuid "84d7f054-fed4-524c-b241-f09c7fd42e5e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060940 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" :tutorial/id #uuid "7aa753a7-1450-53dd-8e61-71e07952cecb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3df97c2e-febb-5f72-be71-e1d739dbbd92" :step/math-academy-id 17183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059580 :question/id #uuid "836f2f1b-5dd4-5dba-81cc-eb9ffe7db8d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4693" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1afe82-86ba-5a15-9037-74717692ecb2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671586} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671585}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To a Coefficients Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "46eeafc2-7d22-58a7-bc12-127e3c86f554" :step/math-academy-id 17184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059581 :question/id #uuid "2ff0a6f5-f3b2-5b39-8b69-1a0c969548cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4694" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c27a3f3c-77c7-55e9-8714-1f24c4a48063" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630310} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186628667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639186}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "797229e4-e202-5744-a0d3-6e8c4cae4059" :step/math-academy-id 17185 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059582 :question/id #uuid "a24c9621-793b-5c60-9c47-2b452f0faa2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4695" :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "691b9107-a935-5965-b3ac-0f3a193ce9bd" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526154} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639244}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform One Matrix Into Another"} :step/id #uuid "cfe1d425-2185-5011-8249-58d7c6bd7ec8" :step/math-academy-id 17186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059583 :question/id #uuid "f8417bbc-b748-519b-a997-fbb4aed755eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4696" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0464b51e-8568-521e-96a5-a65c04569c2a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629462} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186629456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639188}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform a Given Entry to a Desired Value"} :step/id #uuid "0daa33bf-3f45-5650-9a9c-808f765e3f26" :step/math-academy-id 17187}] :activity/title "Elementary Row Operations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059883}]}
